          "message_id": "0e0560a8-2396-4dde-a1a3-c772fa103d37",
          "content": "You're learning C pointers from Beej's Guide. I analyzed your project (three pointer programs: address-of, dereference, passing pointers to functions) and we built a `lessons/` folder with five progressive lessons (box/label, aliasing, pass-by-value vs. pointers, swap, array/pointer decay), a `capstone_scores.c` tying them together, and a `lesson5b_decay_matters.c` demo. We then dug deep into array-to-pointer decay: arrays carry their size only in the compiler's type (via `sizeof`), that info is lost when passed to a function, so you must pass a separate length — and compared this to Rust slices (`&[T]`), which are fat pointers bundling pointer + length. Next up would be lesson 6 on NULL and `malloc`/`free`.",
