      "assistant": {
        "Response": {
          "message_id": "d6f76ce1-72c8-4afa-a73c-b5d66bf490ed",
          "content": "Good catch to question it — this trips people up. The answer has two parts:\n\n**As filenames: yes, totally fine.** `01_address.c` is a perfectly legal filename. The operating system and the compiler don't care what a file is named. You can compile it with no problem:\n\n```\ngcc 01_address.c -o address.exe\n```\n\nThe number prefix is purely for *you* and your file browser — it has zero effect on the code.\n\n**As C identifiers: no.** This is probably what's nagging you. Inside C code, *names you write in the language* — variables, functions, etc. — cannot start with a digit:\n\n```c\nint 01count;      // ERROR - identifier can't start with a number\nint count01;      // fine - number is allowed, just not first\n```\n\nSo the rule you're half-remembering is real, but it applies to **identifiers inside the code**, not to **filenames**.\n\n**One gotcha where they'd collide:** `#include`. If you ever `#include` a file, the filename just sits inside quotes as text, so numbers are still fine:\n\n```c\n#include \"01_helpers.h\"   // fine - it's a string, not an identifier\n```\n\nBut there's a subtle trap with header *include guards*, which are identifiers:\n\n```c\n#ifndef 01_HELPERS_H      // ERROR - macro name can't start with a digit\n#define 01_HELPERS_H\n```\n\nSince your lesson files are standalone `.c` programs (each with its own `main`), you won't hit this — you're not including them anywhere. So numbered filenames are safe for your project. If you ever add shared headers, just name the *guard* without a leading digit (e.g. `HELPERS_01_H`) even if the filename starts with a number.",
          "thinking": {
