      "assistant": {
        "Response": {
          "message_id": "d372b073-8da9-4545-8b8c-78199d785168",
          "content": "Good eye — that `*` in `int *findFirst(...)` is the part that confuses almost everyone at first. It's the **return type**, not part of the name.\n\nRead the declaration like this:\n\n```c\nint *findFirst(int *arr, int n, int target)\n```\n\nGroup it as `int *` + `findFirst(...)`:\n- `int *` → \"this function returns a **pointer to an int**\"\n- `findFirst(...)` → the function name and its parameters\n\nSo `findFirst` doesn't return an `int` — it returns the *address* of an int. That's exactly why inside it you see `return &arr[i];` (an address) and `return NULL;` (the \"no address\" value), never a plain number.\n\n## Why it's written `int *findFirst` and not `int* findFirst`\n\nBoth are legal — the compiler ignores the spacing. These three are identical:\n\n```c\nint *findFirst(...)\nint* findFirst(...)\nint * findFirst(...)\n```\n\nC programmers usually attach the `*` to the name side (`int *findFirst`) out of habit, because of how it reads in *variable* declarations:\n\n```c\nint *p, q;   // p is int*, but q is just int!\n```\n\nThe `*` binds to the individual item, not the type — so writing it next to the name is a visual reminder. For a function it doesn't matter, but people keep the same style.\n\n## The symmetry that makes it click\n\nNotice this function's return type mirrors how you'd *store* its result:\n\n```c\nint *hit = findFirst(data, 5, 15);\n```\n\n`hit` is declared `int *` (a pointer to int). `findFirst` returns `int *`. They match — the function hands back a pointer, you catch it in a pointer. Then `*hit` dereferences it to read the actual value, same as every pointer you've used so far.\n\nSo: the `*` means \"returns a pointer.\" It's the function-return version of the exact same `*` you already know from `int *p`. Want me to go ahead and add this NULL lesson now?",
