          "message_id": "b943c0e3-759e-44e5-8e2a-f4ba5273caeb",
          "content": "You now have the full pointer arc, from basics through dynamic memory. Here's what lesson 11 adds and why it's the payoff for everything before it.\n\n## What `malloc`/`free` gives you\n\nEvery array you've written so far had its size **baked into the source code** — `int a[10]` is always exactly 10. But real programs often don't know the size until they run (the user says how many items they have, a file has an unknown number of lines, etc.). `malloc` solves that: you ask the system for memory *at runtime*.\n\n## How the four concepts you learned all converge here\n\nThis lesson isn't new magic — it's your existing knowledge combined:\n\n- **Pointers (L1–2):** `malloc` returns a pointer to the block. The pointer *is* your only handle to that memory.\n- **NULL (L10):** `malloc` returns NULL if it can't get the memory, so the `if (arr == NULL)` check is the exact pattern from the previous lesson — now with a real reason to exist.\n- **Array/pointer duality (L8–9):** once you have the pointer, `arr[i]` just works. The dynamically allocated block behaves identically to a normal array because indexing is pointer arithmetic.\n- **`sizeof` (L9):** you ask for `n * sizeof(int)` bytes so the code is correct regardless of how big an int is on the machine.\n\n## The one new responsibility: cleanup\n\nFixed arrays (`int a[10]`) clean themselves up automatically. `malloc`'d memory does **not** — it stays reserved until you `free` it. Two rules:\n\n1. **Every `malloc` needs exactly one `free`.** Forget it and you \"leak\" memory — the program holds memory it's no longer using.\n2. **Set the pointer to NULL after freeing.** Then any accidental reuse hits an obvious NULL (crash you can catch) instead of silently corrupting freed memory — a \"use-after-free\" bug, one of the nastiest in C.\n\n```c\nfree(arr);\narr = NULL;   // now a stray *arr is an obvious NULL, not silent corruption\n```\n\n## The big picture\n\nThis is *why pointers exist as a language feature*. Without them, you couldn't have runtime-sized data at all. `malloc` hands you a region of memory and a pointer is the only way to reach it — there's no variable name attached to it. Everything you learned was building toward being able to manage memory yourself, which is the thing that makes C C.\n\nThat rounds out the pointers chapter well. In Beej, the natural next steps are **`realloc`** (resizing a malloc'd block — e.g. a growable array), then **structs** and **pointers to structs** (the `->` operator), which is where you start building real data structures like linked lists. Want to continue into `realloc`, or move on to structs as a new topic folder (`02-structs/`)?",
