        "Response": {
          "message_id": "ab130c86-90cf-4e45-a27b-e607c68b6f11",
          "content": "You're learning C pointers from Beej's Guide. We built a numbered lesson series in `01-pointers/` (address-of, deref, aliasing, function args, swap, array/pointer decay, NULL, malloc/free, and pointer arithmetic), plus a capstone in `app/`, and reorganized the project with numbered topic folders and a `.gitignore`. Along the way we dug deep into the \"arrays don't carry their length\" idea — why `sizeof` loses the size on decay, why functions need a separate `n` parameter, and compared it to Rust slices (fat pointers). We also ran a benchmark proving copy-by-value (via struct) is ~2x slower than passing a pointer, and demonstrated with real addresses that `arr[i]` is `*(arr + i)` with arithmetic scaled by element size. Next up: structs and `->` in a new `02-structs/` folder.",
