          "message_id": "3a086984-8a11-45d6-813f-fef7e79ca3ec",
          "content": "Both struct lessons are in. Here's what they teach and how they connect to the pointer work you've already done.\n\n## Lesson 1 — struct basics (the `.` operator)\n\nA struct bundles related values into one named type:\n\n```c\nstruct Point { int x; int y; };\nstruct Point a = {3, 4};\n```\n\nAccess members with the dot: `a.x`, `a.y` — for both reading and writing. Two things worth noticing in the lesson:\n\n- **A struct is one value.** `struct Point b = a;` copies *both* members at once, and `b` is fully independent of `a`. This is the by-value copying we exploited in your benchmark — structs copy, raw arrays decay.\n- **Members sit next to each other in memory.** `sizeof(struct Point)` is 8 (two ints), and the whole struct lives at one address with members laid out side by side.\n\n## Lesson 2 — struct pointers (the `->` operator)\n\nThis is where it ties back to everything you learned. When you have a *pointer* to a struct, reaching a member is clumsy with dereference-then-dot:\n\n```c\n(*p).x        // works, but ugly: parens are required\np->x          // identical, and how everyone writes it\n```\n\nThe one fact to memorize: **`p->x` is exactly `(*p).x`.** The arrow is pure shorthand for \"dereference, then take the member.\" (The parens in `(*p).x` are mandatory because `.` binds tighter than `*` — without them, `*p.x` would try to do `*(p.x)`, which is wrong. The arrow exists precisely to avoid that trap.)\n\nAnd the practical payoff, straight from your pointer lessons:\n\n```c\nvoid moveBy(struct Point *p, int dx, int dy) {\n    p->x += dx;\n    p->y += dy;\n}\n```\n\n`moveBy` takes a *pointer*, so it modifies the caller's real struct instead of a copy — same reason `swap` and `curveUp` took pointers. For a two-int struct the copy is cheap, but for a big struct, passing `&a` avoids cloning the whole thing. The exact copy-vs-pointer tradeoff you benchmarked, now with structs.\n\n## Where this is heading\n\nYou now have `.` (struct value) and `->` (struct pointer). The next two lessons build directly on these:\n\n- **`03_passing_structs.c`** — by value vs. by pointer, measured, closing the loop with your benchmark.\n- **`04_linked_list.c`** — the big one: a struct with a member that's a *pointer to the same struct type*. That self-reference, combined with `malloc` and `->`, is how you build linked lists, trees, and graphs. It's the moment all three topics (pointers, malloc, structs) fuse.\n\nWant me to continue with `03_passing_structs.c`, or jump to the linked list since that's the real payoff?",
