          "message_id": "6196994d-ac73-48e8-bf3e-24e398790860",
          "content": "Both struct lessons are now in `03-structs/`. To recap lesson 2 (the one you were missing):\n\n**The arrow operator `->`** is shorthand for dereferencing a struct pointer and accessing a member:\n\n```c\np->x        is exactly        (*p).x\n```\n\nThe parens in `(*p).x` are required because `.` binds tighter than `*`. The arrow exists to spare you that awkwardness — and you'll see `->` far more often than `(*p).x` in real code.\n\nThe practical point: `moveBy(struct Point *p, ...)` takes a pointer, so `p->x += dx` modifies the caller's actual struct rather than a copy — the same pointer-for-mutation pattern from `swap` and `curveUp`.\n\nYou can compile and run it the usual way:\n\n```\ncd 03-structs\ngcc 02_struct_pointers.c -o 02_struct_pointers.exe\n.\\02_struct_pointers.exe\n```\n\nNext up is `03_passing_structs.c` (by-value vs by-pointer, tying back to your benchmark), then the payoff — `04_linked_list.c`, where a struct holds a pointer to its own type and combines with `malloc` and `->` to build your first real data structure. Want me to continue with the linked list?",
