        "Response": {
          "message_id": "145a3a94-c1c5-413e-a814-dbf718f30959",
          "content": "Here's what lesson 3 drives home, and how it closes the loop with your earlier work.\n\n## The core contrast\n\n```c\nvoid tryMoveByValue(struct Point p, ...)   // gets a COPY — can't change caller\nvoid moveByPointer(struct Point *p, ...)   // gets the address — changes caller\n```\n\n- **By value:** `tryMoveByValue(a, 100, 100)` modifies a local copy and `a` is untouched. Same pass-by-value rule you saw with plain ints back in the pointer lessons — it applies to structs too.\n- **By pointer:** `moveByPointer(&a, ...)` reaches the real `a` through `p->x`, so the change sticks.\n\n## The key difference from arrays\n\nThis is the subtle bit worth pausing on: **structs and raw arrays behave oppositely when passed to a function.**\n\n| passed to a function | what happens |\n|---|---|\n| raw array (`int a[]`) | decays to a pointer — *never* copied |\n| struct (`struct Point p`) | copied in full — every member |\n\nThat's why `tryMoveByValue` can even exist. You *couldn't* write the equivalent \"by value\" version for a raw array — it would decay to a pointer whether you wanted it to or not. Structs give you the choice that arrays don't.\n\n## Tie-back to your benchmark\n\nRemember the `Big` struct in `12_copy_vs_pointer_perf.c`? That was *this exact mechanism*. The only reason you could force a full array copy in that benchmark was by wrapping the array in a struct — because structs copy by value. So:\n\n- Small struct (like `Point`, 8 bytes): pass by value freely, the copy is trivial.\n- Large struct: pass `&s` by pointer to avoid the copy cost you measured (the 2.1× slowdown).\n- Need to modify the caller's struct: pass by pointer regardless of size.\n\nSo the rule of thumb in the lesson comment — *by value for small or when you want a copy, by pointer for large or to modify* — is just the benchmark result turned into practical advice.\n\nCompile and run to see it:\n```\ncd 03-structs\ngcc 03_passing_structs.c -o 03_passing_structs.exe\n.\\03_passing_structs.exe\n```\n\nNext is the payoff lesson: `04_linked_list.c`, where a struct contains a pointer to its *own type*. That self-reference plus `malloc` plus `->` is how you build a linked list — the first real data structure, and the moment pointers, dynamic memory, and structs all come together. Ready for it?",
          "thinking": {
